## 13. STRICT MODIFICATION RULES

These rules are mandatory.

### Rule 1 — Inspect Before Editing

Never modify a source file without first understanding its purpose and dependencies.

### Rule 2 — Trace Dependencies

Before changing a shared utility, hook, service, type, or API contract, identify all affected consumers.

### Rule 3 — No Blind Refactoring

Do not perform broad automated replacements without validating the resulting implementation.

### Rule 4 — No Speculative Deletion

Do not delete files, state, functions, or dependencies based solely on naming or superficial usage searches.

Verify actual reachability and dynamic usage.

### Rule 5 — Preserve Existing Functionality

Every change must be evaluated against the documented features and actual implementation.

### Rule 6 — No Fake Fixes

Do not suppress errors, disable checks, weaken TypeScript settings, or bypass security to obtain a passing build.

### Rule 7 — No Unnecessary Rewrites

Do not rewrite entire modules when a smaller, safer correction achieves the objective.

### Rule 8 — No Unsupported Assumptions

Discover actual file locations, implementations, package versions, scripts, and architectural relationships from the repository itself.

Never invent file paths or assume that a documented file still exists.

### Rule 9 — Protect Sensitive Information

Never expose, overwrite, or unnecessarily inspect secret values from environment files.

Preserve environment variable contracts.

### Rule 10 — Verify Every Significant Change

Use appropriate static analysis, tests, and build verification after modifications.

### Rule 11 — Maintain Architectural Consistency

A correction in one module must not create inconsistent behavior in another module.

### Rule 12 — Make Actual Changes

You are authorized to inspect, refactor, correct, and optimize the existing application source code.

Do not stop after presenting recommendations.

Implement safe, justified improvements directly.

